# Service Rules — Angular 21

Follow all rules in `AGENTS.md`. Additional scoped rules for services:

## Providers & State
- Core services: `providedIn: 'root'` (singleton)
- Feature services: `providedIn: 'root'` or scoped to a feature route's providers
- Expose state as signals; use `computed()` for derived state
- Inject dependencies with `inject()`

## Async Data & HTTP
- `httpResource()` for reactive GET endpoints with signal-based parameters
- `resource()` for custom async operations with signal-based inputs
- Centralized `ApiService` for mutations (POST/PUT/DELETE)
- Functional interceptors registered via `provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([...]))`
- `HttpClient` is auto-provided in v21 — never import `HttpClientModule`
- Type all API request/response payloads with interfaces

## Caching & Interop
- Cache with signal-based patterns (cache result in signal, invalidate on mutation)
- Use `toSignal()` / `toObservable()` for signal/observable interop
- Keep service API clean: expose signals and methods, not internals

## Naming (v21 style guide)
- Prefer responsibility-driven names: `OrderApi`, `AuthStore`, `CartTotals` in `order-api.ts`
- Legacy `.service.ts` only when matching existing convention

## Dependencies
- Never call other feature services directly — go through core services
- Inject only what you need (avoid large dependency graphs)

## Anti-patterns
- No direct `HttpClient` usage from components (use `ApiService`)
- No class-based interceptors (use functional)
- No exposed mutable state
- No duplicate error handling (interceptors own it)
- No `HttpClientModule` imports
